  "enhanced_information": "The case presents a 16-year-old woman with primary amenorrhea, normal secondary sexual characteristics, and normal estradiol and testosterone levels. The gynecological examination reveals vaginal agenesis and ultrasound shows absence of the uterus, while the ovaries are normal. The absence of the left kidney suggests a developmental anomaly. This clinical presentation is consistent with Rokitansky syndrome (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome), characterized by congenital absence of the uterus and vagina, often associated with renal anomalies. The reasoning chains correctly identify Rokitansky syndrome as the most likely diagnosis, supported by the absence of androgen excess, which rules out congenital adrenal hyperplasia and polycystic ovarian syndrome. Morris syndrome is also ruled out due to the presence of normal secondary sexual characteristics. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is Rokitansky syndrome.",
  "analysis": "The clinical presentation of primary amenorrhea in a 16-year-old woman with normal secondary sexual characteristics, normal levels of estradiol and testosterone, agenesis of the vagina, absence of the uterus, and normal ovaries suggests a diagnosis of Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome, also known as Rokitansky syndrome. This condition is characterized by the congenital absence of the uterus and vagina, while ovarian function remains intact, which aligns with the findings in this case. The absence of the left kidney is also consistent with the renal anomalies that can accompany this syndrome. Other options such as Morris syndrome (Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome) would typically present with abnormal testosterone levels and secondary sexual characteristics, while congenital adrenal hyperplasia and polycystic ovarian syndrome would not explain the absence of the uterus and vagina. Kallman syndrome primarily affects the sense of smell and is associated with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, which does not fit this case. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is Rokitansky syndrome.",
